<p style="margin-top: 1cm; font-size: 300%;"><strong>Memory of Blood Interview:</strong></p>
<p class="title-special title-box">This interview was released 2005/11/25.</p>
<p style="margin-top: 0.4cm; font-size: 200%;">Translator: <a data-astro-prefetch="load" href="/credits/people/barnabism"><u>Barnabism</u></a><br>Source: <a data-astro-prefetch="load" href="/series-guide/ths-guide/dod-2-memory-of-blood"><u>here.</u></a><br>Original link: <a href="https://www.tumblr.com/dod2-mob-tl-project"><u>here.</u></a></p>
<p class="para"><b>WARNING:</b> I didn't receive explicit permission for this, like the other translations, as Barnabism has seemingly disappeared off of the internet. I considered us friends, so I'm gonna keep this up until further notice as I believe they would've been okay with it.</p>
<div class="over"><img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/01.jpg?updatedAt=1783903093631"></div>
<p class="title-special title-box">Pictured: Taro Hasegawa, Takamasa Shiba, Akira Yasui, and Kimihiko Fujisaka</p>
<p class="para">We asked the four main staff about their feelings towards this work and a never-before-seen look into the stories regarding its development!</p>
<p style="margin-top: 0.4cm; font-size: 165%;">The Concept of Drakengard 2</p>
<p class="title-special title-box"><br>&mdash;It's been about four months since the release of
Drakengard 2 (Which from here on will be referred
to as "2"), but what has the response been from its
players?<br><br></p>
<p class="para"><b>Mr. SHIBA</b> &mdash; (Title omitted hereafter): There seems to be mixed
opinions about the story and the characters, but the overall
response isn't too bad.</p>
<p class="para"><b>Mr. YASUI</b> &mdash; (Title omitted hereafter): Yes, the base concept for
2 was to make it an easy-to-follow game. In 1, the story
branched out from the middle, making to difficult for some
players to grasp the content, and there were parts in where it's
production team deliberately left things out to make the setting
more difficult to understand. In order to improve upon these
issues in 2, we used language that was as easy to understand as
possible, and created a story that followed in a straight path.
Even so, this ease of understanding has cause a bit of
controversy among players.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;What do you mean by that?</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; For those who played and enjoyed the dark and vague
atmosphere of 1, 2 seemed a bit too simple to them. It doesn't
seem like "Drakengard" to them.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; On the other hand, for people who were going to be
introduced to the series the first time through 2, I thought it
would be better to make it as easy to understand as possible for
them. As far as the story is concerned, I didn't want it to be as
dark as 1, but rather incorporate the taste of traditional RPGs
into a style and setting like Drakengard's.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;How early did the initial plans for Drakengard 2
start?</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; Quite early! Immediately after the release of 1, I was
already trying to convince the staff.</p>
<p class="para"><b>Mr. HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; (Title omitted hereafter): Shortly after 1 was
released, I remember feeling a little bit excited to see what
appeared to be a proposal for 2 on the desk of a high-ranking
person at the company. I was thinking to myself "Yes, let's do
this again!"</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; At first, I never would have imagined that I would be
given the responsibility of directing the project.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; When I spoke to him about it, he was really surprised!
(Laughs)</p>

<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; It was something that had never crossed my mind! But
thanks to him, I think I was able to fill in almost all the parts
that didn't fully live up to expectations in 1, or that I couldn't
talk about.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;If one thing's for certain, it's that the story ended
in a way that could be considered "A perfect
resolution."</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; There are many RPG sequel titles that follow the
atmophere of the original work, even as the story and main
characters change completely. Of course, while that's a perfectly
valid line of thought, it was something I definitely didn't want to
do with the Drakengard series.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; The main premise when producing this sequel was to
bring the story to a conclusion. It was decided from the very
beginning that it would take place in a time when the characters
from the previous work were still around.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; One of the earlier concepts was to have either Manah or
Angelus serve as the main character.</p>
<p class="para"><b>Mr. FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; (Title omitted from here): In the very early stages,
there was a plan to have Angelus be the main character. The
story would have been about raising Angelus, who would be
reincarnated as a young girl in her next life. Unfortunately, this
plan got rejected as its atmosphere was far too different than
that of 1's. So, personally speaking, I was a little happy to see it
revived as an initial concept project for this artbook materials
collection.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;As a big fan of 1, I would also have liked to see
something like that idea<small><sup id="note-a1"><a href="#note-a2"><span style="color: #6AC3F2;">(1)</span></a></sup></small> for Drakengard.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; There were many other concepts as well. As a matter of
fact, we even went to a conference camp <small><sup id="note-b1"><a href="#note-b2"><span style="color: #6AC3F2;">(2)</span></a></sup></small> together to come up
with a plan! We did this with 1, as well. However, that one was ...
Well? To be honest, it was pretty difficult, wasn't it?</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; Oh, it was definitely pretty intense. (Laughs) But the
conference camp for 2 was held at a certain training centre in
Zushi, in the Kanagawa Prefecture. It was a really isolated island,
we thought, "Is this really still the Kanagawa Prefecture !? "</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; However, the location wasn't too great, either. When
I was there, I felt like I was still somehow trapped within the
company.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; That's because the person in charge of organising the
event was Iwasaki<small><sup id="note-c1"><a href="#note-c2"><span style="color: #6AC3F2;">(3)</span></a></sup></small> (Laughs). I was the organiser for the
boarding house when it was the first game.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; Well, thanks to all of you I was able to concentrate on
the planning conferences all the way through! Because of that, I
feel like I was able to come up with some ideas. I think it was at
that training hall that 2 really started to take off.</p>
<div class="over">
<div style="background-color: #373737;   border: thick solid rgba(135, 135, 135, 0.6); max-width: 80vw; margin: 4%;">
<div class="over"><img style="max-width: 50vw; margin: 12px;" src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/02.jpg"></div>
<p class="para">Square Enix's 10th Development Division and 9th Producer Development Divison</p>
<p class="title">柴 貴正&nbsp;&nbsp;Shiba Takamasa</p>
<p class="para" style="font-size:20px;margin: 16px;">"We aimed to make the game easier to
understand so that it could be enjoyed by
everyone."</p>
</div>
</div>
<p style="margin-top: 0.4cm; font-size: 165%;">The Main Character Whose "Normalcy" is "Different"</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;Among the many unique characters that appear in
the Drakengard series, Nowe, the main character,
feels quite different, doesn't he?</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; The character of Nowe is quite unlike any other character
within Drakengard, yes.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; He's very "normal". Many characters in Drakengard are
"Different", for better or for worse, but Nowe is the only one who
sets himself apart in his normalcy.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; His overall character is very modernistic, and I think
that's why it's so widely said that he doesn't fully feel like he's
from the Drakengard series.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; It's because Nowe is such an ordinary-feeling character
that he begins to feel out of place when surrounded by so many
unordinary characters. If anything, even though he's supposed to
be the most normal one of the cast, he ends up looking the
strangest! But even so, it seems this ended up being a
disappointment to fans who were expecting a protagonist like the
first Drakengard. He seems to be hated quite a bit, and I feel sorry
for him. Ahh, but there is a part of me that intended to make his
character that way.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;What do you mean by that?</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; I think the beginning of his story is quite "Empty". Rather
than fighting out of his own will, he seems to be heavily influenced
by those around him. He fights because his superior, Gismor, tells
him to fight. And then he meets someone he cares about like
Manah, who says she's going to break the Keys to the Seal, so he
helps her. As the Newbreed, he possesses some of the strongest
powers in the series, but he wields his sword without having found
a reason to fight &mdash; when you think about it, that's a horrifying
concept.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; I was hoping players would feel unsettled and averse to
these aspects of Nowe. That Nowe, who simply fights and does as
he's told without thinking too deeply about the right or wrong or
even his reason for fighting, might actually be the worst person of
the cast.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; There were some parts of Nowe's personal development
that couldn't fully be fleshed out in the game alone, but Ms.
Eishima did a wonderful job of laying it out in the novel adaptation.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; For those who are interested, I would recommend reading
the novel adaptation to experience the true charm of Nowe's
character.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; On a similar note, doesn't Nowe's personality
have some similarities to Yasui's? Could this be the
fate of all the Drakengard protagonists? (Laughs)</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; Oh, they're similar! I thought so too. Caim in
1 is like Yoko<small><sup id="note-d1"><a href="#note-d2"><span style="color: #6AC3F2;">(4)</span></a></sup></small> and Nowe is like Yasui. (Laughs)</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; Is that so? Well, if I'm being honest, I actually
thought that several times when I was developing him. I
often worried that it would end up like that.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;Maybe that's why Yasui has such a deep
attachment to Nowe. So in that case, what
do you think of the game's heroine, Manah?</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; Manah, in contrast to Nowe, had a rather
smooth development. Her character was already
well-established in 1, and by the time of 2, she hasn't
changed much.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; As the story progresses, you'll see that even
by the time 2 takes place, the "Nameless being = God"
still exists within her heart. She's a person with serious
mental instability who's being treated as if she's a saint
and is fighting in a war. There are parts in the story
where I wanted players to feel the sense of danger and
discomfort to be had in that. But as you might guess,
she doesn't seem to be well-liked by fans, just like
Nowe...</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; When we were working on the story and
characters, Shiba and I decided on the main image from
the start: "The main character grows from a boy to an
adult while being guided by a beautiful older woman" -
something like that.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; Guided by Manah, who is like an older-sister
type character, Nowe, a young boy still unable to
decide his own path in life, grows little by little. The
story of 2 is also a story about the growth of this boy.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; Nowe, who was always being guided by Manah,
eventually finds a reason to fight of his own will. Not
for something grand like "Protecting the harmony of
the world", but something very personal&mdash;his love for
Manah. I think that facet of human nature is something
very typical for Drakengard as a whole.</p>
<div class="over">
<div style="background-color: #373737;   border: thick solid rgba(135, 135, 135, 0.6); max-width: 80vw; margin: 4%;">
<div class="over"><img style="max-width: 50vw; margin: 12px;" src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/05.jpg"></div>
<p class="para">Cavia Development Headquarters &mdash; Development Department &mdash; Creative Director</p>
<p class="title">安井 章&nbsp;&nbsp;Yasui Akira</p>
<p class="para" style="font-size:20px;margin: 16px;">"Nowe is like my alter ego, so
I'm very attached to him."</p>
</div>
</div>
<p style="margin-top: 0.4cm; font-size: 165%;">The Side Characters Who Support the Story</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;What about the other heroine, Eris?</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; The concept of "the Goddess" already existed from the
start, but other than that, she didn't have much of a strong
personality.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; At first, she lacked a lot of the impact Manah had. This
actually wasn't just an issue with Eris, but at some point every
character was given the order to "inject more poison." Well ... I
was the one who ordered it. (Laughs)</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; We say "Poison", but because there was a larger cast
than in 1, each character's individuality tended to be weakened.
The order was to do something about that.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; And the demon girl who was set to burn Manah at the
stake was the result. I think that the main essence of Drakengard
is that the story is woven together by characters who have a deep
darkness hidden within their hearts like that.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;When you think about it, the cast who makes up
the Knights of the Seal, the main antagonists, are all
characters that are very typical of the Drakengard
series.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; That may be so. As a matter of fact, its the antagonists
who are sometimes more popular than the main characters. The
secret to that popularity might lie in the fact that they're
especially easy to understand and have a more extreme
personality.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; Not to mention, they're all pact-partners, so there's no
way they could have normal personalities. (Laughs)</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; Take for example the antagonist General Gismor &mdash; he
has a look that at first glance makes it easy to tell that he'll be a
boss later down the line. In a sense, he symbolises what we
wanted to do with 2. The very concept of 2, "Easy to
understand", is reflected in the points of his character.</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; Similarly, when I designed Yaha, I designed him to be
a typical narcissist &mdash; a character who would probably go and
describe their own self as "Beautiful". But even though his
character is supposed to be one who is attracted to both men
and women in the story, people who are attracted to both
genders in real life are usually not of this type. Like Gismor, he
also largely captures the image of "Bewitching".</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;Hanch's design also has good taste.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; hanch seems to be popular, doesn't she? I wonder
why that is?</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; She's a character I had a lot of trouble designing. It
was exceptionally difficult to express the concept of a woman
who was originally beautiful, but lost her charm as a result of
her pact. As a last resort, I tried giving her certain features
like dark circles under her eyes and the detail that she was
always dripping with water.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; This idea of always being soaking wet was very popular
among the production staff. Maybe it was attraction instead?</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; Just to mention, it's not spoken of in the game, but
Hanch actually has an older sister who is even more beautiful
than her and a nice personality. She's the type of woman that
people really like. Hanch, on the other hand, was so cute when
she was younger that she was called "Sunsmile". However,
this was due to her cunning and careful calculation. She was,
for one reason or another, jealous of her older sister, who
always emitted a dazzling and radiant charm. My interpretation
is that such ferocity towards her sister was further amplified
due to her pact, and that her inner charm was lost, rather than
her outward appearance.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;It seems that Hanch, Yaha, and Zhangpo each
have their own character colours of blue, yellow,
and red. Why so?</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; This resulted from more of an issue with the game
than of design. It was known from the start that these three
characters wouldn't appear in any FMVs. Because of this, we
were able to set the colours quite freely, since if a character
with colours that were too bright were to appear in a cutscene,
that character would unnecessarily stand out or the overall
colour would have to be changed. It would prove difficult to
put these things together. On the other hand, if the colours or
designs were too subdued, it wouldn't look good on the screen
at all. There were a lot of things I had to consider because of
this.</p>
<div class="over">
<div style="background-color: #373737;   border: thick solid rgba(135, 135, 135, 0.6); max-width: 80vw; min-width: 60vw; margin: 4%;">
<div class="over"><div class="Guide" id="shiba"><img style="max-width: 30vw; margin: 12px;" src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/06.jpg"><img style="max-width: 30vw; margin: 12px;" src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/07.jpg"></div></div>
<p class="title">Yasui's Work</p>
<p class="para" style="font-size:20px;margin: 16px;">He was the main designer
of the previous game, and
the director of this one. He
was deeply involved in the
construction of the world of
Drakengard 2.</p>
<p class="para" style="font-size:20px;margin: 16px;">Furthermore, 2 will mark
his debut as director! His
hobby is watching movies,
but he tends to pay more
attention to the direction
and blocking before getting
too absorbed in the story.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="over">
<div style="background-color: #373737;   border: thick solid rgba(135, 135, 135, 0.6); max-width: 80vw; margin: 4%;">
<div class="over"><img style="max-width: 50vw; margin: 12px;" src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/08.jpg"></div>
<p class="para">Cavia Development Headquarters &mdash; Development Department &mdash; Graphic Designer</p>
<p class="title">藤坂 公彦&nbsp;&nbsp;Fujisaka Kimihiko</p>
<p class="para" style="font-size:20px;margin: 16px;">"We paid special attention to the
design of the characters and the use of
colours."</p>
</div>
</div>
<p style="margin-top: 0.4cm; font-size: 165%;">About Your Favourite Characters and Scenes</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;From here, we'd like to ask everyone about their
favourite characters and scenes. Mr. Shiba, would you
like to go first?</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; My favourite character is Gismor. First of all, he has the
best design. I want to give my praise to Fujisaka, that is to say, I
think this character is the only one who got OK'ed at the first shot.
After all, Fujisaka's best characters are the old men. (Laughs)</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;Tachiki Fumihiko's voice also fits the character
perfectly!</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; As expected from a professional like Tachiki, I think it's
thanks to his rough voice that the whole game came out so solid. Of
course, I like the character as well! I think it's cool how he sticks to
his beliefs. That is, even if they are evil.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;What is your favourite scene?</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; Angelus' and Caim's final scene, of course. I liked it so
much that I took the scene when it had already been completed and
reconstructed it to make it even more moving!</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; At first, it wasn't a scene that required much effort. There
weren't any flashback scenes from 1, and the two of them
disappeared even more abruptly. But, now I think it would have
been nice to have made that scene even more moving.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; For the fans who have been playing since 1, this was the
one part that was going to make them cry, so we put a lot of effort
into it!</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;What is your favourite scene?</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; As you might expect, I definitely feel an attachment
towards Nowe. There are some parts of his character that are
similar to me &mdash; I like the final battle against Legna! That scene
where the parent and child talk and fight while expressing their
feelings to each other is one of my favourite scenes in the series.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; At first, that scene was much simpler.</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; It was flat, by which I mean, the lines didn't come out
very well. The emotional dialogue at the end came far too late, and
the story had already been wrapped up and resolved by then.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; That was one of the dilemnas: The better the player, the
faster they could defeat the boss. In the meantime, we had
calculated the estimated battle time when creating it, but it still
relied entirely on the skill of the player.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;Next up, what do you think, Mr. Fujisaka?</p>

<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; I agree with Yasui. I also quite like Nowe! But the
best one is definitely the Kelpie! Hasegawa's design was
amazing!</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; Eh, really? That's the first time I've heard
something like that. (Laughs)</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; Really. I really like Ifrit, too.</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; Ifrit doesn't even appear in the game! (Laughs)</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; That sounds an awful lot like a Fujisaka thing to say ...
(Laughs)</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;Do you like the monster designs?</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; Yes. I should say, the design of Ifrit was made
during the production of the first game, so I have a certain
fondness for it.</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; In the end, it was shelved for 1, but it finally saw
the light of day in 2, which made me happy. The design was
quite elaborate, with the hand having a cannon that looked
something like a kamado, so when I finished it, I thought "I
did it!!"</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; However, Zhangpo was killed without it even
having a chance to appear. (Laughs)</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; Yes, that's the role it played in the end, and I
was disappointed in the way it was handled. Zhangpo was too
weak...</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;What do you think, Mr. Hasegawa?</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; As far as human characters go, I think it would
have to be Urick. Koyama Rikiya's voice is very gruff, and I
think it suits the character. And even though the design is
unbalanced, it's still in good taste.</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; Eh, what do you mean?</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; Although the area around his upper body is tight,
his head looks huge because of his mask. That sort of
imbalance is really cool. If I'm being honest, I get the feeling
that Fujisaka likes those kinds of designs, but he hasn't had
much opportunity to draw them until now.</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; You're right! I was very particular about that mask.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; It's interesting how suddenly he takes off that mask.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;Admittedly, I was quite surprised, as I thought
there would be more build-up towards Urick's
true identity.</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; There's nothing rough about it, and I rather like
it's graciousness. It's honestly a favourite of mine. As for
monsters, I like the Holy Dragons! Unlike Legna and Angelus, I get the image of them being
somewhat soft.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; If we're talking about monsters, then I really like
Unknown. Especially the hanging Unknown you fight during
aerial combat!</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; The spinning attack makes it excellent.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; I think the story from Chapter 10 onwards became
much more exciting thanks to the appearance of that
monster. The impact it left was breathtaking!</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; I wanted to somehow create an enemy that was
comparable to the child angels from the previous game.
Thanks to all of you, I think we were able to create a creepy
enemy with a different approach!</p>
<div class="over">
<div style="background-color: #373737;   border: thick solid rgba(135, 135, 135, 0.6); max-width: 80vw; min-width: 60vw; margin: 4%;">
<div class="over"><div class="Guide" id="shiba"><img style="max-width: 30vw; margin: 12px;" src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/09.jpg"><img style="max-width: 30vw; margin: 12px;" src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/010.jpg"></div></div>
<p class="title">Fujisaka's Work</p>
<p class="para" style="font-size:20px;margin: 16px;">He doesn't just design
characters for the series, but
also handles a wide variety of
designs across the board, from
the world setting to weapon
designs and clothing. His
attention to detail and detailed
designs are said to sometimes
bring graphic artists to tears.
Even after the software release,
he was one of the people who
were involved with 2 for the
longest time, and has made
illustrations for both the novel
adaptation and the original
illustrations for this book.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="over">
<div style="background-color: #373737;   border: thick solid rgba(135, 135, 135, 0.6); max-width: 80vw; margin: 4%;">
<div class="over"><img style="max-width: 50vw; margin: 12px;" src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/011.jpg"></div>
<p class="para">Cavia Development Headquarters &mdash; Development Department &mdash; Graphic Designer</p>
<p class="title">長谷川 太郎&nbsp;&nbsp;Hasegawa Taro</p>
<p class="para" style="font-size:20px;margin: 16px;">"Even though Ifrit finally got to see the light
of day, I was disappointed to see Zhangpo
get defeated so quickly."</p>
</div>
</div>
<p style="margin-top: 0.4cm; font-size: 165%;">About the Concepts for the Next Installment</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;So here, I would like to ask you all about your
ideas for the next game, which fans are probably
very interested in...</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; I don't have anything to say about this yet. To tell
the truth, if I were to make a new Drakengard game, I have
an idea for the kind of form I would like to make it in. When
I went overseas for work not too long ago, I suddenly got
the idea on the plane. However, that doesn't actually mean
the project's been put into production at all so far...<br>
Unfortunately.</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; Personally, I feel like I've exhausted all my ideas
for Drakengard with 2.</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; We all worked on the production of 2 with great
enthusiasm, determined to deliver the last great sendoff for
the series. That said, if we were to make a sequel, it might
have to be a completely new version of Drakengard.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; If that's the case, how about a Drakengard with no
dragons?</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash; That would mean less work for me. (Laughs)</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; It's just a joke, don't worry. But, we started working
on 2 really soon after finishing the previous game. If you
think about it like that, I feel like there needs to be some
kind of recharging period, even if it's just for a while. After
all, even if I say I want to make something new, it can't get
started unless all the production team goes "Let's do it!"</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; Hey hey, no one said they didn't want to! (Laughs)</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;If you were to make a sequel, you'd have to hold
that "Conference camp" again, right?</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; If we were, I'd definitely be on board with it. But if so, I
wonder if it would be better if I was the organiser ...</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; I would really like that. (Laughs)</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;Finally, do you have a message you would like to
give to the fans?</p>
<p class="para"><b>YASUI</b> &mdash; I'm really, really happy that there are so many people
getting involved. I hope that people will continue to love the
Drakengard series even more.</p>
<p class="para"><b>FUJISAKA</b> &mdash; I'm grateful that even though it's been a long time
since its release, that there are still passionate fans who
continue to buy the novel and this collection of concept
materials. Everyone's comments are very encouraging!</p>
<p class="para"><b>HASEGAWA</b> &mdash;I'd be happy if this book allows some to see the
detailed concepts that aren't usually published in magazines,
and makes them love Drakengard even more.</p>
<p class="para"><b>SHIBA</b> &mdash; This series, which I've been involved in for about the
last five years, has come to an end for the time being.
Personally, I think it's all thanks to the fans that this has
become a title I feel so fondly of. That said, I think it would be
great if I could see all those fans again sometime. I don't know if
it will be through a sequel, a completely new work, or if any of
that will actually even happen. Until then, I would like everyone
to play 2 to its fullest.</p>
<p class="title-special title-box">&mdash;Thank you all very much!</p>
<div class="over">
<div style="background-color: #373737;   border: thick solid rgba(135, 135, 135, 0.6); max-width: 80vw; min-width: 60vw; margin: 4%;">
<div class="over"><div class="Guide" id="shiba"><img style="max-width: 30vw; margin: 12px;" src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/012.jpg"><img style="max-width: 30vw; margin: 12px;" src="https://ik.imagekit.io/closesttopurple/bg/013.jpg"></div></div>
<p class="title">Hasegawa's Work</p>
<p class="para" style="font-size:20px;margin: 16px;">The one behind all the
various monsters and dragons
that appear. He created a
dragon specific to Drakengard
which stands out from most
types of dragons that appear
in other games. Additionally,
the monster designs, who
have a unique charm you just
can't bring yourself to hate,
stems from his unique sense
of style. He says that because
he was able to leave
production as soon as
possible due to the nature of
his work that he was always
able to enjoy 2 in a fresh way.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p style="margin-top: 0.4cm; font-size: 165%;">Notes</p>
<p class="para"><sup id="note-a2"><big><a href="#note-a1"><span style="color:#6AC3F2;">1</span></a></big></sup>: Point of production with the initial concepts submitted to the "Drakengard 2" planning
meeting. The special project at page 108 in this book is based off these initial concepts.</p>
<p class="para"><sup id="note-b2"><big><a href="#note-b1"><span style="color:#6AC3F2;">2</span></a></big></sup>: Development camp... A conference camp where the development team gathered to
go over their plans. During the production of the previous game, Mr. Shiba served as the
organiser for this plan and rented a guesthouse, which has since become a tradition. The
conference organiser for this work was Mr. Iwasaki.</p>
<p class="para"><sup id="note-c2"><big><a href="#note-c1"><span style="color:#6AC3F2;">3</span></a></big></sup>: Mr. Iwasaki Takuya... He is the general manager of development and chief producer
of Cavia Co., Ltd. Like Mr. Shiba, he is heavily invested in the development of the
Drakengard series.</p>
<p class="para"><sup id="note-d2"><big><a href="#note-d1"><span style="color:#6AC3F2;">4</span></a></big></sup>: Yoko Taro... Created the atmosphere of the series alongside Mr. Shiba and Mr.
Iwasaki. While he participated as the creative director for 1, he served in 2 as a video
editor.</p>

